text:
{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C2",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It is the only direction rooted in a real, verifiable product truth: the hidden seam allowance in well-made couture. That makes the longevity promise credible, ownable and hard for competitors to copy, which matters on a topic where luxury is often accused of greenwashing.",
      "It covers the whole topic. Refitting and repair serve first owners, and re-gifting and reuse serve later owners. C1 and C4 each cover only one side, and C3 covers only resale.",
      "It is highly platformable. The 'inch' can be a visual device, a unit of measure, a film motif, a service name and a product-design standard. This gives the campaign a long life beyond a single launch, unlike a one-off spectacle.",
      "It reframes luxury as a long-term relationship with a garment rather than a purchase event. This fits Dior's obsession with fit and the atelier, and it avoids a sustainability lecture, so it protects brand desirability.",
      "It gives people something to do: book a refit, take a piece in or let it out, or pass it on. This turns the idea into behaviour and service, not only a message.",
      "Its main risks can be managed. The claim can be backed by a published construction standard and atelier capacity. The metaphor can be stretched to leather goods and shoes through equivalents such as resoling, re-edging and re-strapping, framed as 'the next inch'. Emotional storytelling about real owners and real refits can offset the dryness of the technical detail."
    ],
    "rejected": [
      {
        "id": "C1",
        "reason": "It is bold and press-worthy, but it is a one-off spectacle with little sustained behaviour change and no ongoing service or offer for repair and reuse. It also risks seeming to romanticise damage and devalue expensive pieces, which could clash with Dior's craft and quality positioning. It depends on client consent and creative-director buy-in, so it carries high execution risk for a narrower payoff. Its best element, real owners' emotional stories, can be borrowed into C2."
      },
      {
        "id": "C3",
        "reason": "It gives a tangible reuse offer, but it is a business-model decision more than a campaign idea. It needs heavy internal alignment and risks cannibalising new sales and diluting exclusivity. Visible previous-owner history raises privacy concerns for clients. It also addresses resale only and does little for repair or the first owner's relationship with the piece. It works better as a later extension of C2, where 'owner number two' becomes the next inch."
      },
      {
        "id": "C4",
        "reason": "It is warm and beautiful to film and supports employer branding, but celebrating craft heroes is a well-worn luxury trope and risks being forgettable. It tells people about repair without giving them anything new to do or experience, and it is weak on reuse and secondhand. It works better as a storytelling layer inside C2, where the atelier hands perform the refits."
      }
    ]
  }
}

{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C2",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It is the only direction that makes repair and reuse a creative act rather than a service or a business model. That fits a house whose founding story, the 1947 New Look, was about reshaping what already existed. The idea is anchored in Dior's own history, not borrowed from a sustainability playbook.",
      "It sits where Dior's authority is strongest: the ateliers, the archive and the long-lived icons (Bar jacket, Lady Dior). It does not depend on Dior inventing new credibility. It shows craft authority in action.",
      "Repair becomes desirable, not defensive. A runway moment, a film series and editorial content give it the cultural spark and press interest that a quiet aftercare service would not earn. It speaks to fashion audiences in the language they already care about: silhouette, reinvention and craft.",
      "It is platformable. The seasonal rhythm gives a repeatable structure (call for pieces, atelier transformation, runway, film, client events) that can run for years and keep generating fresh stories. Each season's cohort brings its own owners, memories and garments.",
      "It reinforces ownership and lifetime value without discounting or pushing second-hand. Clients are encouraged to keep and reinvent pieces, which supports the brand's pricing power and avoids the cannibalisation risk that comes with a resale model.",
      "Its main concerns can be managed. Capacity limits can be handled by treating it as a curated seasonal event alongside a permanent, scalable atelier repair and alteration service. That also answers the 'one-off stunt' risk. Specific, verifiable impact proof (pieces kept in use, hours of atelier work, materials reused) can be published each season to guard against greenwashing. Scarcity is also consistent with luxury, provided selection is transparent and open to clients across regions and eras."
    ],
    "rejected": [
      {
        "id": "C1",
        "reason": "Dior Lineage is commercially smart and strong on provenance and anti-counterfeit, but it is more an operating model than a campaign idea. It needs heavy data and logistics infrastructure before it can say anything culturally. It also invites the resale-cannibalisation and brand-devaluation debate, which puts Dior on the defensive. It works better as a later enabling layer, for example giving transformed C2 pieces a lineage record, than as the lead idea."
      },
      {
        "id": "C3",
        "reason": "The Mended Star is an appealing, ownable symbol with warm roots in Dior's lucky star, but it is a graphic device more than a platform. It risks encouraging deliberate damage for the mark, which would undercut the longevity message. Many luxury clients also do not want visible repair on a pristine piece. It could be a small signature detail inside C2, but not the central idea."
      },
      {
        "id": "C4",
        "reason": "Petites Mains Open Hours is provocative and generous and would earn goodwill, but it is the weakest link to Dior's own products and ownership, so the commercial return is unclear. It is costly and capacity-hungry, and it risks looking like a charity stunt or commoditising skill, which could dilute the exclusivity that supports the brand. C2 keeps the spotlight on the artisans while staying tied to Dior's pieces and clients."
      }
    ]
  }
}

{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C4",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It is the only direction that turns repair into a Dior-native ritual. Dior's name was built on the fitting, the moment a garment is made for one body. Second Fitting extends that ritual across a piece's life, so repair feels like couture rather than maintenance. That is the aspirational reframe the topic needs in luxury.",
      "It gives the House an ownable phrase and a repeatable mechanic (the dated, signed label). That is what makes it a platform. Each new life stage (inheritance, a changed body, a new chapter) becomes a fresh chapter across film, boutique, client ledger, social and press, without the idea wearing out.",
      "It covers the whole behaviour of reuse, not one slice. Resizing, recutting, re-edging and restoring cover real reasons people keep clothes, and the story engine of inherited and re-fitted pieces is emotionally richer than a design flourish or a tracking system.",
      "It builds equity where Dior is strongest. Naming the petites mains and showing their hands at work credits the atelier, reinforces craft authority, and gives the House believable proof that it values longevity.",
      "It stays brand-safe and commercially sensible. Keeping the service within Dior pieces protects exclusivity and quality control, unlike C1. Making the kept piece a status act supports desirability and client loyalty, and the service creates ongoing contact between the House and its pieces. Including secondhand owners widens reach without Dior having to run a resale business like C2.",
      "Its main risks can be managed through design: scope atelier capacity and turnaround before launch, tell stories of change and the present (not only heirlooms) to avoid a nostalgic tone, use a light authentication step for secondhand pieces, and use real clients and real atelier work so the stories do not feel staged."
    ],
    "rejected": [
      {
        "id": "C1",
        "reason": "It is a generous and surprising stunt, but it works as a one-off event rather than a platform. It is costly with little commercial return, it invites the 'PR gesture' reading, and it carries quality-control and liability risks. Repairing other brands' clothes could also dilute Dior's exclusivity, and it builds no ongoing relationship with Dior's own pieces. Its best element, the human story behind a garment, is captured more sustainably in C4."
      },
      {
        "id": "C2",
        "reason": "Provenance is a smart luxury idea, but it is primarily a resale and data infrastructure play. It risks cannibalizing new sales, makes pre-owned highly visible in a way that can undermine exclusivity, and demands heavy authentication and platform build. It also leans transactional and tracking-led rather than emotional, so it is weaker as a campaign idea. Its useful parts, such as the record of a piece's life and the dated label, are folded into C4 without the resale burden."
      },
      {
        "id": "C3",
        "reason": "It is highly visual and easy to execute, but visible mending and kintsugi are already familiar in fashion, so it risks feeling derivative. It addresses only owners who want a visible mark, may glamorize damage, and reads as a design trend that will age quickly rather than a lasting behaviour shift. It is better treated as a possible finishing option within C4 than as the central idea."
      }
    ]
  }
}

{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C3",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It is the only direction that gives Dior a reason to talk about repair and reuse now. The New Look's 80th anniversary supplies a cultural hook, a founding myth and a hero moment that the other three lack. Inverting the 1947 gesture (abundance answering scarcity, now radical luxury is what already exists) is a provocative idea that is still strategically grounded.",
      "It puts Dior's strongest asset, the couture atelier, at the centre of the story. The highest craft in the house is applied to the reuse of existing garments, so reuse reads as the pinnacle of luxury and not as a sustainability concession. That is credible for Dior and hard for a competitor to copy.",
      "It is built on emotion. A garment someone cannot let go of, its story, and the owner wearing the remade piece on the runway turn reuse into memory and meaning. This gives the campaign human content that earns attention beyond fashion media, and the final image of owners closing the show is a distinctive, ownable ending.",
      "It is platformable. Submissions, documentary films of the process from first cut to final fitting, the show, an exhibition and boutique services can all grow from one idea. It can also absorb the best elements of the other candidates: a signature repair mark from C4 on remade pieces, a provenance record from C1 on each remade garment, and atelier skills content from C2 as supporting material.",
      "Its main weaknesses can be fixed within the design. The risk of elitism and greenwashing is handled by adding a broader repair and alteration tier in boutiques, announced at the same moment, so couture remakes are the showcase and not the whole offer. Clear rules on accepting non-Dior garments (for example, a limited number for the couture selection and Dior-only for the service tier) settle the eligibility question. The limited lead time is manageable because submissions, selection and film production can be phased, with the show as the culmination and the service tier continuing afterwards."
    ],
    "rejected": [
      {
        "id": "C1",
        "reason": "Lineage is a strong long-term loyalty and authentication system, but it is infrastructure more than a campaign. It needs heavy data and operational build-out, raises privacy questions about naming owners, and lacks a dramatic hero moment. It could also push people towards resale over repair and cannibalise new sales. It works better as a later supporting layer, such as a provenance record on remade and repaired pieces, than as the lead idea."
      },
      {
        "id": "C2",
        "reason": "Petites Mains, Open Hands is generous and humanises the artisans, but it does not drive repair and reuse of Dior's own products, which is the behaviour the brief needs. It would be hard to scale beyond a few cities and risks feeling like a charity project or diluting the mystique and exclusivity the house depends on. Its content works better as a supporting chapter inside C3 than as the central idea."
      },
      {
        "id": "C4",
        "reason": "The Visible Mend is visually striking and gives owners a reason to repair, but visible mending in the spirit of kintsugi is a known idea in fashion, so it risks feeling borrowed and not distinctly Dior. It could be read as a styling trend rather than a lasting change in behaviour, and some luxury clients prefer invisible repairs, so the mark has to be optional. It has no anchoring cultural moment. It is more valuable as a detail within C3, such as a signature mark on remade pieces, than as the campaign platform."
      }
    ]
  }
}

{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C1",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It is the only direction that changes how a luxury owner sees a repaired piece. It turns a mend from a admission of damage into a mark of status, and that inversion is both surprising and strategically sound. Repair and reuse stop being a duty and become a source of desire.",
      "It is the most ownable idea. A 'second signature' stitched by Dior's own hand is something resale platforms, third-party menders and competitors cannot offer without the maison's atelier, craft codes and name. It also gives a reason to repair with Dior rather than elsewhere.",
      "It carries Dior's strengths of couture, thread, needle and human hands straight into the product and the client's wardrobe, so the brand connection is natural rather than imposed.",
      "It is highly platformable. Macro photography, editorial imagery, a limited signed-mend mark, owner stories and the possibility of the mark becoming a collectible code can all grow from one visual idea, and the mend itself is a physical object people will photograph and share.",
      "Its concerns can be managed. Perfection clash can be handled by making the mend an optional, couture-level gesture (clients choose invisible or signed repair). Kintsugi resemblance can be avoided by staying in Dior's own vocabulary of thread, needle and atelier marks. Atelier capacity can be handled by launching with a capsule of iconic pieces (Bar jacket, Saddle bag, Lady Dior) and a waiting-list model that adds exclusivity.",
      "It can absorb the best elements of the other directions without taking on their risks: real owner stories from C3 as imagery, and named petites mains from C4 as the signers of each mend."
    ],
    "rejected": [
      {
        "id": "C2",
        "reason": "Bold and headline-making, but it asks the maison to give up a new collection season, which clashes with commercial calendars and revenue targets. Bespoke re-cutting is very costly to deliver at scale, and altering iconic pieces risks purist backlash and devaluing archival items. It also treats reuse as a transformation of the garment, whereas C1 achieves a similar creative desire with less operational and brand risk by preserving the original piece and adding to it."
      },
      {
        "id": "C3",
        "reason": "Emotionally strong and useful for CRM and authenticity, but it is better as an infrastructure layer than as a lead idea. It needs a registry or passport with significant technology and data-privacy work, similar provenance initiatives already exist among luxury peers, and heirloom storytelling risks feeling nostalgic to younger audiences. It does not change how people feel about a repaired piece in the way C1 does. Its multi-generational stories can be used inside C1 instead."
      },
      {
        "id": "C4",
        "reason": "Easy to produce and authentic, but atelier-showcase content is a familiar luxury trope, so it is the least ownable and least surprising of the four. It celebrates craft rather than changing owner behavior around repair and reuse, and it relies on technicians being willing and permitted to appear publicly. It works better as a supporting content layer for C1, with named repairers signing the mends."
      }
    ]
  }
}

{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C2",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It rests on the sharpest insight of the four: the most emotional document of early working life is also the ugliest and least understood. That tension between feeling and format gives the campaign a clear point of view that rivals would struggle to copy.",
      "It is the best fit with the B2B, SaaS and productivity category. The buyer is the employer or HR team, and the action is concrete: customise the template and send it with the first pay. That puts Canva inside onboarding workflows, which neither a consumer meme nor a seasonal gesture can do.",
      "It is Canva's core skill, turning complexity into clarity, applied to a document nearly everyone has received. The brand claim feels earned rather than borrowed.",
      "It keeps the traditional first-job emotion the client raised, which C1 narrows away. Its emotional core is the first proof that your work counts, shown through a document everyone remembers.",
      "It is easy to dramatise. A before/after of a stub works in film, social, PR and out-of-home, and a human story (a first-time earner finally understanding what they earned) gives it an emotional anchor.",
      "It can grow into a platform. The same approach extends to offer letters, benefits sheets, performance reviews and onboarding packs, so it can become 'the human side of work documents' rather than a one-off.",
      "Its main risks are manageable by design. Keep the template to presentation and plain-language labelling, with no tax or financial advice, and add a clear disclaimer that payroll data and accuracy stay with the employer's payroll provider. Ship integration-ready layouts and partner with HR and payroll platforms to reduce the adoption dependency. Make the personal note and human storytelling the lead in communications so the idea doesn't feel dry."
    ],
    "rejected": [
      {
        "id": "C1",
        "reason": "It has strong product relevance and a clear route to Pro and Teams, but it reads 'paycheck' too narrowly and leaves out the traditional first-job emotion the client raised. Invoice and quote templates are a crowded, utilitarian space where Canva has no distinct point of view, and the idea is less culturally surprising. Freelancer first-invoice stories could work as a later extension or supporting story of C2."
      },
      {
        "id": "C3",
        "reason": "It has high emotional pull and is easy to make, but gratitude toward parents and mentors is familiar territory for many brands, so it lacks distinctiveness. It has a weak B2B angle and little relevance to workplace buyers. It is likely to read as a seasonal stunt rather than a platform. Its playful stub-style visual is better borrowed as a supporting activation for C2 around payday or graduation season."
      },
      {
        "id": "C4",
        "reason": "It is playful, nostalgic and a good UGC engine, but its link to B2B and workplace outcomes is loose, so it generates designs without building business value. It depends on participation and could stall without a seed community, and its trend-style meme format risks a short life. It is better kept as a possible social or PR layer than as the core campaign."
      }
    ]
  }
}

{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C4",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It is the only direction built on a genuine, revealing insight: people forget the number on the first paycheck but remember the work they made to earn it. That reframes a money milestone as a creative milestone, which is territory only Canva can credibly own.",
      "It is a platform, not a one-off. The 'First Paid' receipt is a repeatable, ownable format that can run as a user-generated gallery, creator seeding, press features, workplace and school activations, and local versions in different markets.",
      "It has the strongest shareability. Each receipt pairs a real artifact (the menu, the invitation, the first deck) with a date and a payer, so it carries nostalgia and pride and gives people a reason to post and tag the person who paid them. C1 and C3 offer far less to share, and C2 is cold by comparison.",
      "It is inclusive across Canva's audience: freelancers, employees, founders, students and side-hustlers all have a first piece of paid work. This avoids the employer-only limit of C1 and the freelancer-only limit of C3.",
      "It produces authentic proof for B2B credibility. A public gallery of real work that earned real money shows Canva's role in livelihoods and careers, which is more persuasive to teams and enterprise buyers than a feature push, and it can feed case studies and sales stories.",
      "It celebrates the user's creation rather than the tool, which turns 'Made in Canva' from a watermark into a badge of a career beginning. That is a strong brand-building position for a category that is usually functional and dry.",
      "Its main risks can be managed. Seed the gallery before launch with creators, Canva staff and well-known figures to avoid an empty-room start. Make the amount optional or blurred by default and let people choose how much to show to limit privacy and comparison issues. Widen the definition of 'work' to include presentations, flyers, posters, résumés and pitch materials, plus a 'first thing I made for someone' entry, so those whose first paycheck did not come from design still have a way in."
    ],
    "rejected": [
      {
        "id": "C1",
        "reason": "Clear B2B relevance, but it reaches employees only through employers, so it has little public resonance or earned-media potential and excludes freelancers and creators. It also risks feeling like a corporate gimmick unless the gesture is sincere. Its best element, the team-signed card and recap, can be borrowed later as an activation or B2B extension of C4 without being the central idea."
      },
      {
        "id": "C2",
        "reason": "It offers a credible PR hook and a participation route through open templates, but survey-led 'index' stories are a familiar format and unlikely to surprise. Data captures the cost of living rather than the emotion of the milestone, and it carries higher production cost and methodological scrutiny. It also lacks C4's ownable, repeatable user-generated mechanic. Its data could support C4 as a later layer, for example the most common first paid work by city."
      },
      {
        "id": "C3",
        "reason": "It has concrete product utility and measurable template usage, but it narrows the audience to freelancers and side-hustlers and excludes anyone getting a first salary. It competes with dedicated invoicing tools, so it reads as a feature push, and the invoice is not an inherently shareable or emotional object. It works better as a supporting template series within C4 than as the campaign idea."
      }
    ]
  }
}

{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C4",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It finds a fresh, ownable insight: a first paycheck is proof of ability, not a salary figure. That moves the conversation from money, which is awkward and invites comparison, to pride in the work, which people like to show off. Canva can credibly own the idea of 'work worth being paid for'.",
      "It is the strongest fit for Canva's category. It frames Canva as the tool behind real, paid work (menus, pitch decks, flyers, logos, social calendars), which supports B2B, SaaS and productivity positioning. It shows the professional quality of Canva output without turning into a product demo.",
      "It keeps the emotional universality of a first paycheck that C1 loses and C3 only partly captures. Interns, gig workers, freelancers, small-business owners and first-time employees can all take part, because the story is about the work and the feeling, not the payslip.",
      "It is platformable beyond a single execution: a shareable template and UGC format, OOH featuring real first work, a creator series, employer and school onboarding, and a Teams or small-business tie-in. It has a longer life than a one-off seasonal stunt.",
      "Hiding the amount by default avoids the privacy risk of C2 and the regulatory and accuracy risk of C3, and it reduces pay-gap comparison. Its main weaknesses can be managed: widen 'work' to include non-visual first jobs through the story or feeling (the flyer for a shift, the rota, the handwritten menu), and seed it with real creators and small businesses so it does not read as a generic hashtag."
    ],
    "rejected": [
      {
        "id": "C1",
        "reason": "It is practical and commercially clear, but it narrows 'first paycheck' to the self-employed and loses the broader emotional pull of the topic. Invoice and rate-card templates are a crowded, utility-led space where Canva has no distinctive voice, and the idea is unlikely to spark organic sharing. Its best element, the shift into being a professional, is absorbed into C4, which also reaches freelancers while staying inclusive."
      },
      {
        "id": "C2",
        "reason": "It is warm, easy to grasp and a good template demonstration, but it is weak on B2B and productivity relevance and has little to say about Canva's role in work. Payslips hold sensitive personal data, so encouraging people to share them creates a privacy risk. It is also a nostalgic, one-time, seasonal use case with limited platform life. C4 keeps the keepsake emotion without those problems."
      },
      {
        "id": "C3",
        "reason": "The insight is real, since payslip confusion is universal, but the idea pushes Canva into financial education, where it has little authority and faces legal and accuracy risk. Tax systems differ by region, which limits global scalability. It risks a lecturing tone and relies on partners, so the role for Canva's product is less ownable. It may work later as a supporting content or partnership layer, but it is not strong enough to lead."
      }
    ]
  }
}

{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C2",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It is the only direction built on a real, revealing insight rather than a sentiment: the first paycheck often pays for work nobody hired you to do, and that work is frequently making things look good. People recognise it instantly ('me, in my first job'), which gives it natural shareability without relying on the sentimentality that is a cliché for this topic.",
      "It fits Canva's strategic need. It repositions Canva from a student and hobbyist tool to a workplace tool for the non-designer, and it supports the Teams and Enterprise story by showing that design is an everyday work skill, not a specialist function.",
      "It is platformable. New line items, roles, industries and career stages can be added over time ('Line Item: Not in the Job Description' becomes a repeatable format), so the idea can run well beyond a single moment.",
      "It has a practical, product-led payoff. The 'skills receipt' and 'make the case for your next paycheck' template move the audience from recognition to action (a review, a raise pitch, a promotion case), showing Canva in a real work use case rather than as a decorative add-on.",
      "Its main risks can be managed in execution. Framing the 'invisible line item' as a skill worth celebrating and a strength to the employer, not a grievance about unpaid labour, keeps employer buyers on side. Anchoring the hero content in the first job and first paycheck, and sourcing line items from varied industries (not just marketing), addresses the concerns about topic tie and truth across sectors."
    ],
    "rejected": [
      {
        "id": "C1",
        "reason": "It is simple and visual, but it is a one-off gimmick with little platform depth. It leans on the sentimental first-paycheck trope that the category already overuses, has weak ties to Canva's B2B and productivity positioning, and handling real pay stub data creates privacy and trust risks that outweigh the shareability."
      },
      {
        "id": "C3",
        "reason": "The user-generated content potential is real, but the idea is familiar because many brands run success-story campaigns. Implying that a Canva document got someone hired creates a claims risk, and it speaks to job seekers rather than to teams and workplaces, so it does little for the B2B objective."
      },
      {
        "id": "C4",
        "reason": "It is a fresh angle with clear relevance to SMB team-plan acquisition, but the audience is narrow and the idea has less cultural stickiness and social virality. It also risks drifting into HR and payroll territory where Canva has no credibility. It would work better as a later extension or a targeted B2B spin-off of C2 than as the lead idea."
      }
    ]
  }
}

{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C3",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It is the only direction that starts from a real, universal and unglamorous truth about the first paycheck: the excitement of the number collides with deduction shock and a document nobody can read. That tension is a genuine insight, not a seasonal sentiment.",
      "It makes Canva's core belief demonstrable: design is clarity. A before-and-after of a confusing payslip beside a clear one proves the product's value in a single image, which makes it highly shareable and easy for press and social to carry.",
      "It sits squarely in Canva's B2B and productivity category. Employers, HR and payroll teams get a concrete reason to bring Canva into onboarding, internal comms and employee-facing documents, which gives the campaign a commercial outcome that can be measured through template adoption and Teams sign-ups.",
      "It speaks to two audiences with one idea: new earners get emotional and practical value (understanding and a keepsake), and employers get a better employee experience. This gives it more reach than C2 and more substance than C4.",
      "It is a platform, not a one-off. The same logic extends to tax statements, benefits summaries, pension updates and bonus letters, so the idea can run for years as 'making the confusing clear'.",
      "Its concerns are manageable by design. Financial-guidance risk can be handled by keeping explanations descriptive (what each line is) rather than advisory. Privacy can be handled with a redaction-first, on-device or template-only approach for individuals. Payroll access can be handled by launching with templates and a small number of pilot employers or providers, with the individual restyle route working independently of partners.",
      "It is lighter and safer to execute than C2 and more distinctive and business-relevant than C1 and C4, giving the best balance of cultural relevance, brand fit and feasibility."
    ],
    "rejected": [
      {
        "id": "C1",
        "reason": "It has clear B2B logic but reads as a product bundle rather than a cultural idea. It offers little emotional pull for new employees, overlaps with existing HR-software onboarding offers, and is unlikely to generate attention or conversation. Its most useful element, the payslip explainer, is stronger as the focus of C3, which can later grow into a wider onboarding kit."
      },
      {
        "id": "C2",
        "reason": "It is provocative and credible, but operationally heavy, with sourcing, payments, contracts and legal compliance across markets, and it carries real reputation risk if payment, quality or fairness goes wrong. It also reaches only aspiring designers rather than first-time earners in general, and it leans toward Canva's creator ecosystem more than its B2B and productivity positioning. The risk and cost are disproportionate to the audience reached."
      },
      {
        "id": "C4",
        "reason": "It is emotional and simple to run, but it has little connection to Canva's B2B and productivity positioning and sits in familiar thank-you card territory, so it lacks distinctiveness. It is mostly a seasonal template push rather than a platform, and it avoids the real tension of the first paycheck instead of resolving it. Its gratitude and keepsake element can be borrowed as a sharing feature within C3 instead."
      }
    ]
  }
}

{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C2",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It is the only direction where the campaign is the product in action. Turning a confusing pay stub into something clear and friendly demonstrates Canva's core promise (making complex information understandable for anyone) rather than just claiming it.",
      "It is built on a universal, slightly funny truth: 'where did my money go?' The gross-to-net shock is something almost every first-time earner has felt and talked about, so the idea is naturally shareable without forced virality mechanics.",
      "It reaches both audiences that matter. First-time earners get the emotional payoff of finally understanding their paycheck. HR teams and small business owners get a practical template use case, which fits Canva's B2B, SaaS and productivity positioning better than the consumer-only C3 and C4.",
      "It is platformable. 'Design is how things make sense' can stretch across makeover reveals, creator explainers, employer toolkits and OOH that mimics ugly pay stubs, and it can keep running beyond a single moment.",
